  "Sleep and exam anxiety in first-year students: a fictional teaching paper\nThis paper is invented by the Appuscholar team for practice. The study, the people and the numbers do not exist.\n\nAbstract\nFirst-year students often report poor sleep in the weeks before examinations. We tested whether one week of a fixed bedtime routine lowered exam anxiety. Sixty first-year students from one college were randomly placed in a routine group or a waiting group. Exam anxiety was measured before and after the week. The routine group showed a larger fall in anxiety than the waiting group, t(58) = 2.36, p = .022, d = 0.61. Sleep quality and anxiety change were weakly related, r(58) = .31, p = .016. The sample was small and came from one college, so the result needs repeating.\n\nIntroduction\nExam periods are linked with short sleep and high anxiety in students (Nair & Brook, 2019). Earlier work has mostly been observational. Students who sleep badly report more anxiety, but these studies cannot say which comes first. However, very few studies have asked whether changing sleep habits for a short time changes anxiety before an exam. Little is known about what a low-cost routine, which a college could offer to every student, can achieve in one week. Rao and Dutta (2020) found that late bedtimes predicted lower marks, but they did not change bedtimes. The gap this study addresses is the lack of a controlled test of a short bedtime routine. We therefore asked whether a one-week bedtime routine lowers exam anxiety compared with no routine. We expected the routine group to show a larger drop in anxiety.\n\nMethod\nDesign. We used a randomised two-group design with a measurement before and after the week. Random assignment was chosen so that differences between the groups would be due to chance, and a waiting group was used because withholding the routine for one week carried little risk.",
  "Participants. Sixty first-year students (38 women, 22 men; mean age 18.7 years, SD = 0.8) were recruited by notice board posters in one college. Students with a diagnosed sleep disorder were not included. The sample size of 60 was set by the number of students who volunteered before the deadline; no power calculation was done.\n\nMaterials. Exam anxiety was measured with a 12-item scale, the Exam Worry Scale (invented for this paper), rated from 1 to 5. Higher scores mean more anxiety. Internal consistency in this sample was good (Cronbach's alpha = .86). Sleep quality was rated with one question, scored from 1 (very poor) to 10 (very good). We chose a short scale because students were completing it during busy weeks.\n\nProcedure. Students signed a consent form and completed the scale on paper. A coin toss decided the group for each student. The routine group were asked to go to bed within thirty minutes of a fixed time each night for seven days and to switch off screens one hour before. The waiting group were asked to keep their usual habits and were offered the routine after the study. Both groups completed the scale again on day eight. The college ethics committee approved the study. Students were told they could stop at any time.\n\nData processing. Paper forms were entered into a spreadsheet by one researcher and checked by a second. Two students did not return for the second measurement and were removed, leaving 58 for the analysis. Scale scores were the mean of the 12 items. Change scores were calculated as the score after minus the score before.\n\nStatistical analysis. Change scores of the two groups were compared with an independent-samples t-test. The relationship between sleep quality and anxiety change was tested with a Pearson correlation. The significance level was .05, two-tailed. Cohen's d was reported as the effect size. We did not test for normality.",
  "Results\nTable 1. Mean anxiety change (after minus before) by group. Routine group (n = 29): M = -0.52, SD = 0.71. Waiting group (n = 29): M = -0.12, SD = 0.62. A negative change means lower anxiety. The routine group showed a larger fall in anxiety than the waiting group, t(58) = 2.36, p = .022, d = 0.61. Figure 1 shows the two group means with their ranges. Sleep quality rated after the week was weakly related to anxiety change, r(58) = .31, p = .016, so students who rated their sleep better tended to show a smaller fall in anxiety.\n\nDiscussion\nThe findings suggest that a short bedtime routine can lower exam anxiety within one week. This is consistent with the idea that sleep and worry affect each other. The fall of half a point on a five-point scale is modest, but it was reached with a simple routine.\n\nLimitations\nThe sample was small and came from one college, so the result may not hold for other students. Anxiety was self-reported and students knew which group they were in, which may have shaped their answers. We did not measure sleep with a device. The follow-up was one week, so we do not know whether the effect lasts into the exam itself. We also did not check that the groups were similar at the start.\n\nRecommendations\nColleges could offer a bedtime routine before examinations at little cost. Future studies should use larger and more varied samples, include a check of sleep with a device, and follow students through the examination.\n\nConclusion\nA one-week bedtime routine was linked with a larger fall in exam anxiety than no routine in this small sample. The result needs repeating before it is used widely.\n\nReferences\nNair, A., & Brook, T. (2019). Example paper A: Sleep and exams. Journal of Teaching Examples, 1(1), 1-10. (invented)\nRao, S., & Dutta, P. (2020). Example paper B: Bedtimes and marks. Journal of Teaching Examples, 2(1), 11-20. (invented)"
